  "notes": "Miami's brash trade-off duo, modelled on the fast Miami-bass-inflected feel rather than the half-time cuts: 95–105 straight with the clap on 2 and 4, cowbell and timbale accents, and an 808 that behaves like percussion. ⛔ `chords.impliedByRiff` is set because the research says these records frequently carry **no chord instrument at all** — a single riff plus 808 — and the gang-chant hook is not a melody either. The chord part's variety therefore lives in `chordDurationBeats` [4,16], three `harmonicRhythm` values and a 34-semitone voicing register; adding families would be inventing harmony the records omit. ⚠ `bass808.slideProb` is only 0.25 against trap's 0.5, which is the \"almost a bassline-as-percussion role\" line turned into a number — fast repeated 16ths on the tonic, `staccato`, with short glides rather than the sliding sub trap inherits. ⚠ `confidence` medium, and the INFERRED parameters are the internals: the research is medium-high on the bounce-tempo family and only medium on production, so the kick figures (`syncopation` 0.58, density [3,6]) and the perc lane choice are read off \"bouncy with 16th pickups\" and \"Miami-bass hand-percussion and cowbell\", not off a session account. ⚠ The 130–145 half-time cuts the research also names are not modelled here; they are what the two solo entries (`jt`, `yung-miami`) carry between them. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
